1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gregorio and Buglio, Pedro. Duke of Tremistere (I), in the Kingdom of Sicily. Palermo (Italy), c. 1600 - 8.XI.1659. Knight of the Order of Santiago, regent of the Council of Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Carlos de Gregorio, a native of Palermo, and Agata Buglio, a native of the city of Catania, who was a widow at the time of this marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT His paternal grandparents were Pedro de Gregorio and María Ram, both natives of Palermo, and his maternal grandparents Andrea Buglio, Baron de la Bifara, a native of the city of Mineo, and Antonia de la Valle, a native of Catania.
2 CONT
2 CONT After completing his studies and graduating with a doctorate in Jurisprudence, he began his services in the career of the toga as a rational master of the court of the Royal Heritage of Sicily and, finding himself in the service o
2 CONC f this job, as he had, by way of purchase, the land of Tremistere with a population of more than two hundred fires, he requested that he be granted the title of duke and, upon consultation of the Council of Italy (August 17, 1647)
2 CONC , his request was granted, releasing him the corresponding privilege (September 13, 1647).
2 CONT
2 CONT Two days later, upon further consultation with the Council of Italy (September 15, 1647), he was promoted to the post of natural regent of the kingdom of Sicily in the Supreme Council of Italy for the vacancy left by the death of 
2 CONC Antonio Xirota and already in the following year his dispatches were given to him (February 5, 1648).
2 CONT
2 CONT He remained in the town and court serving the aforementioned regency for three years, because once Lucio Denti died he was granted the presidency of the Great Court of the Kingdom of Sicily (May 23, 1651), replacing him in the Cou
2 CONC ncil the Messinian Ascanio Ansalone .
2 CONT
2 CONT He embarked for Sicily in the port of Alicante with his wife and children, and in the course of the journey he was captured by the French and a ransom of 8,000 ducats had to be paid for his freedom and thus, finally, he was able t
2 CONC o take possession of the presidency, in which he remained until his death, after which he was succeeded by Diego Marotta.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fonts and Lit .: General Archive of Simancas, Secretariats Provincial leg. 1469; National Historical Archive, military orders, Expedientillos, 3,124; Santiago, exp. 3617.
2 CONT
2 CONT G. di Blasi, Storia dei Vicerè chronologic, luogotenenti and Presidenti of the Regno Sicily, Palermo, Stamperia Oretea, 1842, pp. XI, XX and XXXVI.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://dbe.rah.es/biografias/113086/pedro-gregorio-y-buglio
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
