1 NAME Giovanni /Beccadelli di Bologna/ Cavaliere
2 GIVN Giovanni
2 NSFX Cavaliere
2 _MARNM Beccadelli di Bologna
2 NICK Ioannes de Bononia
2 NICK Ioannes de Bononia Miles
2 NICK Giovanni di Bologna
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE BEF 1470
1 OCCU
1 TITL Magnifico
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -http://www.genmarenostrum.com/pagine-lettere/letterab/beccadelli_di_bologna.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT   C2. [ex 2°] Giovanni, 
2 CONT
2 CONT armato Cavaliere dal Re d’Aragona e successivamente creato Nobile con il privilegio di inquartare l’arma della casa d’Aragona il 28-IV-1450 (esecutivo il 29-V-1450), Capitano di Palermo nel 1450, Maestro Razionale del Real Patrimo
2 CONC nio, Pretore di Palermo nel 1454, 1455, 1465, 1466 e 1467.
2 CONT                      a) = ………..
2 CONT                      b) = ………..    
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT GIOVANNI, figlio secondo di Giacomo, che fu il primo figlio di Nicolò, fu egli huomo di gran virtù, e governo assai amato dal re Alfonso, dallo quale fu armato cavaliero, e fatto capitano di Palermo l’anno 1450 et anco mastro rati
2 CONC onale del Real Patrimonio, come appare per privilegio, dato in Casale Arnone a 29 d’aprile 13 inditione 1450. Fu pretor di Palermo gl’anni 1454, 1455, 1464, 1465, 1466 e 1467. Hebbe anco molti altri favori, e gratie, carichi, e di
2 CONC gnità in questo regno, e fuori, al quale fu concesso dallo stesso re Alfonso che lui con tutta la sua posterità in perpetuo potessero portare, depingere e scolpire sopra le loro arme, l’arme regie d’Aragona, e delle due Sicilie, c
2 CONC h’all’ hora usava il detto re, come appare per privilegio dato in Casale Arnone a 28 d’aprile XIII inditione 1450. Il qual privilegio fu poi confirmato dall’invittissimo imperatore Carlo quinto a’ nipoti d’esso Giovanni, quando gl
2 CONC i concesse il titolo di don, come si vede per privilegio dato in Ratisbona al primo d’agosto 4 inditione 1546. Hebbe egli con due moglie dodici figli tre con la prima, che furono:
2 CONT Giacomo il primo.
2 CONT Contessa la seconda, che fu moglie di Giovanni Antonio Fuxa, che fu castellano di castel a mare di Palermo.
2 CONT Antonella la terza, che fu moglie di Pietro Antonio di Playa barone di Batticane, il quale fu pretore di Palermo due volte, cioè gl’anni 1502 e 1509. //[f. 11r] 
2 CONT E con la seconda moglie li novi seguenti procreò, cioè:
2 CONT Pietro il quarto, che fu secreto, capitano e pretore di Palermo.
2 CONT Bernardino il quinto che fu prima cantore, e tesorero della chiesa maggiore di Palermo, e dopo fu fatto vescovo di Malta e arcivescovo di Messina
2 CONT Laurea la sesta, che fu moglie di Mariano Agliata dottore, e protonotario di questo Regno.
2 CONT Simone il settimo, che fu secreto, e pretore di Palermo.
2 CONT Giacoma l’ottava, che fu monaca nel monasterio della Martorana, e dopo andò badessa, ò provincialesa perpetua nel monasterio di Valverde in questa città di Palermo.
2 CONT Vincenzo il nono, che fu pretor di Palermo.
2 CONT Andrea il decimo, che morì senza prole.
2 CONT Bartolomea l’undecima, che fu moglie di Antonello Lo Campo baron di Mossumeli.
2 CONT Polidoro il duodecimo, che se ne morì senza figli.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Ad Ioannem fratrem secundo loco genitum sermonem convertamus: videamus, an is suam etiam, ut ceteri, familiam & rebus præclarè gestis, & animi virtute collu strarit?
2 CONT
2 CONT De hoc nihil est, cur dubites, cum semper is haud imparem ingenitæ nobilitati virtutem in rebus omnibus obeundis ostenta rit.  & Miles, & carus Alfonso Regi, quem primum urbis Panormitanæ Ducem, seu ut dictitant, Capitaneum 1450 a
2 CONC c Magistrum Regalis Patrimonii Rationalem in Sicilia, deinde pluries Prætorem iussit anno 1454, 55, 64, 65, 66, 67.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quibus quidem muneribus cum esset ita perfunctus, ut & ferè omnium concitatam de seipso expectationem superarit, & Alfonsum Regem pellexerit ad plerasquè alias tum in Sicilia, tum in Italia provincias ab eodem administrandas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quare sic animum sibi Regis obstrinxit, ut Rex ipsi facultatem summa voluntate concesserit, volveritquè, ut Ioannes in posterum, eiusque posteri familiæ stemmati & Aragoniorum Regum, & utriusque Siciliæ insignia, quibus vel ipse R
2 CONC ex ea tempestate utebatur, superadderet, ijsque de Alfonso Rege benemerentissima Bononiensis familia frueretur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Non hæc fingimus Regiæ Alfonsi literæ loquuntur ecce tibi.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Nos Alfonsus Dei gratia Rex Aragonum, Siciliae,'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Cum vos Egregius & dilectus Consiliarius noster & unus ex Magistres Rationalibus Regni Sicilia ultra Farum IOANNES DE BONONIA, Miles satis, qui clarissimis virtutibus præfulgetis ad conditionem, & bonos, ac laudabiles mores vest
2 CONC ros nostra mentis intuitum, & alias debitum respectum, & considerationem habentes ad grata plurimus fructuosa, & accepta servitia per vos nobis praestita, & impensa quaeue praestatis ad praesens, & præstiturum de bono in melius co
2 CONC ntinuatione laudabili speramus tenore praesentis nostri privilegii Caroli temporibus firmiter valituri de nostra certa Scientia, & consulto vos dictum Ioannem, & totam vestram postertatem & sobolem motu proprio inducti ad nobilita
2 CONC tis gradum attollimus, vosquè sobolem, ac posteritatem vestram omnem in perpetuum honare nobilitatis decoramus.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Volentes & huiusmodi privilegii serie decernentes quam vos sobolis, & posteritati vestre quicumque, & quomodocumque vobis, & illis libenter illis gaudeatis, & gaudere possitus in perpetuum iam in personis, quam in bonis omnibus,
2 CONC  & singulis gratiis, honoribus, muneribus, libertatibus, ac immunitatibus, quibus nobiles, & nobilitatis gradu decorati quomodocumque, & qualitercumque; gaudere sunt soliti, possunt & debent.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Nos.n.vos cum tota vestra progenie & posteritate in praemissis, & circa praemissa gratiose omnen defectum, si quis esset, & reperiri posset ex Regia potestatis plenitudine supplemus, & insuper de scientia, & motu iam dictis ad i
2 CONC mmortalitatis vestrae, & vestrae sobolis, & posteritatis insigne praeconium, eorumdem tenore praesentis vobis, & vestris praedictis damus, confirmamus, & gratiose elargimur arma nostra Regnorum, Aragonum, Siciliae citra & ultra Fa
2 CONC rum, & c. Videlicet signum, quae nos ut Rex Aragonum, Siciliae ultra, & citra Farum, & c. facere, portare in vexillis, & aliis in signis nostris solemus.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Itaque vos & vestri tamquam nobiles, & nobilitatis gradu decorati, & a nobis prædiétis armis insigniti ex nunc in antea, & in perpetuum possitis, & possint dicta arma nostra Regnorum nostrorum portare, eaquè pingi, & sculpiri fa
2 CONC cere ad vestrum libitum voluntatis fuper arma vestra, quæ portare iam soletis, & c Rex Alfonsus.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Integrum si cupis, iam habes in Regio tabulario f. 251. Cuius plane familiæ dignitatem, ac splendorem cum cognosset Carolus V Imperator non illi modo Alphonsi Regis diploma confirmavit, sed eius etiam nomen novis muneribus, ac don
2 CONC is amplificavit cum sanxit ut omnes eius familiæ posteri titulo, DON, & vocarentur, & signarentur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Verum ut tanti viri virtus feruaretur in pofteris divino confilio factum
2 CONT est ut ex co plures nobilitate pares, virtute haud impares extiterint liberi, inter quos præcipuè lacobi, Petri, Bernardini, Simonis, & Vincentii virtus eluxit.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''English Translation''':
2 CONT
2 CONT Let us turn our conversation to his brother John, who was born in the second place: let us see whether he, like the others, has also adorned his family with illustrious deeds and virtue of mind?
2 CONT
2 CONT There is nothing about this, why do you doubt it, since he has always displayed a virtue not unequal to his innate nobility in all matters of conduct. And a knight, and dear to King Alfonso, whom he first appointed Duke of the cit
2 CONC y of Panormitana, or as they call it, Captain in 1450, and Master of the Royal Patrimony Rational in Sicily, then several times Praetor in the years 1454, 55, 64, 65, 66, 67.
2 CONT
2 CONT In which functions he was so accomplished that he surpassed the expectations of almost everyone about himself, and persuaded King Alfonso to administer most of the other provinces both in Sicily and in Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wherefore he so bound the mind of the King to himself, that the King granted him the faculty with the greatest willingness, and desired that John should in future, and his descendants, add to the family's pedigree and the insignia
2 CONC  of the Kings of Aragon and the two Sicilies, which even the King himself used at that time, and that the most deserving family of Bologna should enjoy them from King Alfonso.
2 CONT
2 CONT We do not imagine these things, the letters of King Alfonso speak to you, behold.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''We Alfonso, by the grace of God, King of Aragon, Sicily,''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''With you, our Eminent and beloved Counsellor and one of the Rational Masters of the Kingdom of Sicily beyond Faro, JOHN OF BONONIA, a worthy knight, who shines with the most illustrious virtues in our opinion, and your good and 
2 CONC praiseworthy manners, and having due respect and consideration for the most grateful, fruitful, and accepted services rendered to us by you, and the effort which you are making at present, and which we hope will be made better and
2 CONC  better in a praiseworthy continuation, according to the tenor of our present privilege, Charles, firmly in the times of our certain Knowledge, and having consulted you, the said John, and having been induced by our own motion to 
2 CONC the rank of nobility, we raise you, your offspring, and all your posterity, to the rank of nobility, and we adorn you, your offspring, and all your posterity, with the honor of nobility in perpetuity.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Desiring and decreeing a series of privileges of this kind, which you, your offspring, and your posterity, whoever and however you and they may willingly enjoy, and be able to enjoy in perpetuity, now in persons, as well as in a
2 CONC ll goods, and individual graces, honors, gifts, liberties, and immunities, which nobles, and those decorated with the degree of nobility, in whatever way, and in whatever manner; are accustomed to enjoy, can and ought to enjoy.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''We, you and your entire progeny and posterity, in the premises and about the premises, graciously supply every defect, if any, and could be found, from the fullness of the Royal power, and moreover, from the knowledge and moveme
2 CONC nt already said to the notable proclamation of your immortality, and your offspring, and posterity, by the same tenor of the same present to you and your aforesaid, we give, confirm, and graciously bestow upon you our arms of the 
2 CONC Kingdoms, Aragon, Sicily, on this side and beyond Faro, &c. It is a sign, which we as King of Aragon, Sicily beyond and beyond Faro, &c. are accustomed to carry on our banners, and other signs.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Therefore you and yours as nobles, and decorated with the degree of nobility, and endowed with the arms bestowed by us, from now on and in perpetuity, may, and may carry our said arms of our Kingdoms, and may have them painted a
2 CONC nd engraved at your pleasure of will over your arms, which you are already accustomed to carry, &c King Alfonso.''
2 CONT
2 CONT If you desire it, you already have it in the Royal Archives, f. 251. When the Emperor Charles V fully recognized the dignity and splendor of whose family, he not only confirmed the diploma of King Alfonso, but also enlarged his na
2 CONC me with new gifts and gifts when he sanctioned that all his family should be called and signed by the title, DON, for posterity.
2 CONT
2 CONT But so that the virtue of such a great man might be preserved in the divine court, it happened that from him several sons, equal in nobility and not unequal in virtue, arose, among whom the virtue of James, Peter, Bernardine, Simo
2 CONC n, and Vincent especially shone forth.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.google.com/books/edition/_/DVblPh2i_P4C?hl=en&gl=US&gbpv=0
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
